WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.850
In June of eighteen seventy-six, a cavalry lieutenant colonel ignored his scouts' warning that there were more Sioux in the valley than his men could ever handle, and he split his regiment into three.

2
00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:17.380
A few hours later, of the more than two hundred men riding with him, not a single one was left to tell the tale.

3
00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:24.390
But here is the real story: the greatest victory the Native Americans ever won became the war that cost them their land.

4
00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:43.280
In the spring of eighteen sixty-eight, at Fort Laramie on the plains of Wyoming, representatives of the United States government signed an agreement with chiefs of the Lakota, the Dakota and the Arapaho.

5
00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:48.530
Known as the Fort Laramie Treaty, it brought an end to two years of bloody conflict.

6
00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:56.820
The Oglala Lakota leader Red Cloud had besieged the forts along the Bozeman Trail for so long that the army was finally forced to withdraw.

7
00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:03.030
It was one of those rare moments when an Indian nation made the United States Army accept its terms.

8
00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:11.000
The Fort Laramie Treaty recognized all of the land west of the Missouri River in what is now South Dakota as the Great Sioux Reservation.

9
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:18.490
In the middle of that land stood mountains that rose suddenly from the plains, covered in pine forests, looking dark from a distance.

10
00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:21.500
The Americans called them the Black Hills.

11
00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:27.390
The Lakota called them Paha Sapa, and to them this place was far more than a hunting ground.

12
00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:36.480
It was sacred: a landscape where healing herbs were gathered, where people went on vision quests, where the stories of the people's very existence unfolded.

13
00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:38.850
The treaty was perfectly clear.

14
00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:44.900
Without the Indians' permission, no white person could settle on this land, or even pass through it.

15
00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:50.150
And any future transfer of land would require the signatures of three quarters of the adult men.

16
00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:53.800
That promise held for exactly six years.

17
00:01:53.800 --> 00:02:02.250
In eighteen seventy-three, America plunged into a severe economic depression; banks collapsed and railroad companies went bankrupt.

18
00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:08.940
The country was hungry for a new source of wealth, and rumors of gold in the Black Hills had been circulating for years.

19
00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:15.630
In the summer of eighteen seventy-four, the army organized what was officially called a reconnaissance expedition.

20
00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:20.240
The stated purpose was to find a suitable site for a fort in the region.

21
00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:27.090
At the head of the expedition was the commander of the Seventh Cavalry Regiment, Lieutenant Colonel George Armstrong Custer.

22
00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:34.260
Custer was thirty-four years old at the time and one of the best-known soldiers in the American public eye.

23
00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:45.270
During the Civil War he had become a brevet brigadier general at just twenty-three, and he had made his name with his long golden hair, the uniforms he designed himself, and his reckless cavalry charges.

24
00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:52.280
He was also a writer who loved addressing his readers; his accounts of life on the plains were published in magazines.

25
00:02:52.280 --> 00:03:02.970
The eighteen seventy-four expedition was like a small army: around a thousand soldiers, more than a hundred supply wagons, an artillery battery, scientists, a photographer,

26
00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:06.940
journalists and two experienced gold prospectors.

27
00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:14.110
At the end of July, on the banks of a stream called French Creek, the prospectors saw flecks of gold in their pans.

28
00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:19.360
Custer sent the news by scout to the nearest telegraph line.

29
00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:26.850
Within a few weeks, newspapers on the East Coast were running headlines about great gold deposits discovered in the Black Hills.

30
00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:32.420
The amount actually found was modest, but for a country in depression, that was enough.

31
00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:37.190
The following year, thousands of prospectors poured into the forbidden land.

32
00:03:37.190 --> 00:03:43.880
At first the army tried to remove some of them, but those arriving always outnumbered those being expelled.

33
00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:53.930
In the autumn of eighteen seventy-five, the government brought the Lakota an offer: to buy the Black Hills for six million dollars, or to use them in exchange for an annual lease.

34
00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:56.620
Most of the Lakota refused.

35
00:03:56.620 --> 00:04:03.630
Some said that sacred land was not for sale; others found the offer laughable and demanded a far greater price.

36
00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:11.440
In November of eighteen seventy-five, a secret meeting was held at the White House, attended by President Ulysses Grant himself.

37
00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:13.810
Two decisions came out of it.

38
00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:17.620
First, the army would no longer stop the miners.

39
00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:30.070
Second, every Lakota and Cheyenne band living outside the reservation, in the areas the treaty recognized as hunting grounds, had to return to the agency headquarters on the reservation by January thirty-first,

40
00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:32.760
eighteen seventy-six.

41
00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:36.730
Those who did not return would be considered hostile.

42
00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:40.540
It was an order that was almost impossible to carry out.

43
00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:46.430
In the dead of winter, messengers struggled across snow-covered plains to reach scattered camps.

44
00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:50.720
Some received the news late; some never received it at all.

45
00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:57.730
And the camps it did reach could not march hundreds of kilometers through freezing cold with their children and their elders.

46
00:04:57.730 --> 00:05:02.820
The deadline passed, and the very next day the matter was handed over to the army.

47
00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:08.550
A people who believed they had never given up any land had been declared enemies for living on their own land.

48
00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:11.880
On paper, the army's plan was simple.

49
00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:20.490
Three separate columns would enter the vast basin in what is now southeastern Montana from three directions and close a pincer around the Indian camps.

50
00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:24.620
General George Crook would set out from the south, from Wyoming.

51
00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:28.910
Colonel John Gibbon would come from the west, from the forts in Montana.

52
00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:38.640
And General Alfred Terry would march from the east, from Fort Abraham Lincoln in North Dakota, with Custer's Seventh Cavalry Regiment forming the striking force of his column.

53
00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:42.930
Custer very nearly missed the campaign altogether.

54
00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:55.060
In the spring of eighteen seventy-six, in Washington, he had testified in a corruption inquiry that touched Secretary of War William Belknap, and he had indirectly pointed a finger at President Grant's brother as well.

55
00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:58.230
Furious, Grant stripped him of his command.

56
00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:06.680
Only Terry's intervention allowed Custer to return to the head of his regiment, and even then, command of the whole campaign would remain with Terry.

57
00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:11.770
It is not hard to imagine how eager Custer was to prove himself once again.

58
00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:20.220
Through that winter and spring, families who had broken away from the agencies began to gather around Sitting Bull, the holy man of the Hunkpapa Lakota.

59
00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:23.550
Sitting Bull was then in his forties.

60
00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:29.600
In his youth he had made his name as a warrior, but by now his real weight came from his spiritual authority.

61
00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:35.330
He had refused to sign treaties with the whites and had never settled on a reservation.

62
00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:43.140
In March, the army attacked and burned a Cheyenne camp on the banks of the Powder River, and that drove the Northern Cheyenne toward him as well.

63
00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:51.750
The camp grew as it moved, because with the coming of spring, families leaving the agencies to join the summer hunt had also taken to the trail.

64
00:06:51.750 --> 00:07:00.840
In the first days of June, on the banks of a creek called the Rosebud, the camp held the most important ceremony of the Plains peoples: the Sun Dance.

65
00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:08.490
In this ceremony, dancers prayed on behalf of their people by enduring pain, offering their own bodies as a sacrifice.

66
00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:15.500
That year, Sitting Bull allowed one hundred small pieces of skin to be cut from his arms, fifty from each arm.

67
00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:21.710
Then, covered in blood, without food or water, he danced for hours, gazing at the sun.

68
00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:25.040
Toward the end of the dance, he collapsed.

69
00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:28.690
When he came to, he described the vision he had seen.

70
00:07:28.690 --> 00:07:36.180
He had seen countless soldiers falling from the sky toward the camp, heads down and feet up, raining down like grasshoppers.

71
00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:42.230
A voice had told him that it was giving these soldiers to his people, because they did not know how to listen.

72
00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:49.400
Those who heard the vision interpreted it in only one way: the soldiers would attack the camp, and they would be defeated.

73
00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:52.570
Falling head first was the sign of death.

74
00:07:52.570 --> 00:07:58.620
The vision carried one more warning, and later great importance would be placed on it.

75
00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:05.150
Sitting Bull had told his people not to touch the weapons, horses, or belongings of the dead soldiers.

76
00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:08.960
This gift was for victory alone, not for plunder.

77
00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:12.770
The vision's first test came sooner than expected.

78
00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:21.860
On June seventeenth, Crook's column coming up from the south, roughly one thousand soldiers along with their Indian allies, Crow and Shoshone warriors,

79
00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:28.070
had halted along the upper Rosebud when Lakota and Cheyenne warriors fell upon them in a sudden attack.

80
00:08:28.070 --> 00:08:31.240
Among them was the Oglala warrior Crazy Horse.

81
00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:33.930
The fighting lasted all day.

82
00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:40.940
When the warriors withdrew that evening, Crook held the field, but his ammunition was exhausted and his wounded were mounting.

83
00:08:40.940 --> 00:08:44.910
He fell back to his base camp and did not advance for weeks.

84
00:08:44.910 --> 00:08:50.000
Most important of all, he sent no word to Terry or Custer about what had happened.

85
00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:55.090
And so the southern arm of the pincer was out of the game, and those who remained knew nothing of it.

86
00:08:55.090 --> 00:09:00.340
Meanwhile, the Indian camp moved west, into the valley of the Little Bighorn River.

87
00:09:00.340 --> 00:09:04.470
The Lakota called this valley the Greasy Grass.

88
00:09:04.470 --> 00:09:09.240
As more people arrived from the agencies, the camp stretched for kilometers.

89
00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:17.370
Hunkpapa, Oglala, Miniconjou, Sans Arc, Blackfoot Lakota, and Northern Cheyenne lodges lined the river.

90
00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:25.980
Estimates vary, but the camp held somewhere between six thousand and eight thousand people, among them perhaps fifteen hundred to two thousand men able to fight.

91
00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:30.430
It was one of the largest gatherings the Plains had ever seen.

92
00:09:30.430 --> 00:09:35.680
On June twenty-second, Terry sent Custer and his regiment south along the Rosebud.

93
00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:40.450
Terry himself and Gibbon would swing around by way of the river and come in from the north.

94
00:09:40.450 --> 00:09:46.980
They offered Custer the slow-moving Gatling guns and an extra battalion of cavalry, but he turned down both.

95
00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:54.150
He believed his regiment, some six hundred cavalrymen along with Arikara and Crow scouts, would be equal to anything.

96
00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:59.880
For three days the regiment pushed forward fast; some nights the march went on until morning.

97
00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:08.170
The scouts found the place along the creek where the Sun Dance had been held, and then the broad, trampled trail the camp had left behind as it moved.

98
00:10:08.170 --> 00:10:14.060
At dawn on the morning of June twenty-fifth, the Crow scouts climbed a hill between the two rivers.

99
00:10:14.060 --> 00:10:17.710
The soldiers would come to call that hill the Crow's Nest.

100
00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:26.800
From there, at a distance of more than twenty kilometers, the scouts saw an enormous horse herd and the smoke of campfires in the Little Bighorn Valley.

101
00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:33.490
By the time Custer climbed the hill, the sun was up and the valley was buried in haze; he could see nothing.

102
00:10:33.490 --> 00:10:38.580
Despite his scouts' warnings, he kept the size of the camp small in his mind.

103
00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:46.550
That same morning, word came that soldiers who had gone back to look for a supply box dropped along the trail had run into a few Indians.

104
00:10:46.550 --> 00:10:50.200
Custer took this to mean that the regiment had been discovered.

105
00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:57.370
His greatest fear was not a fight; it was that the village would scatter and flee, and the campaign would come home empty-handed.

106
00:10:57.370 --> 00:11:02.300
So he did not wait for the next day, and decided to attack at once.

107
00:11:02.300 --> 00:11:05.150
Toward noon, he divided the regiment.

108
00:11:05.150 --> 00:11:13.760
Captain Frederick Benteen was to take three companies, about a hundred and twenty-five men, to the hills in the southwest and cut off anyone trying to escape.

109
00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:23.170
Major Marcus Reno, with three companies of about a hundred and forty soldiers and the support of the scouts, was to ride down into the valley and attack the village head-on.

110
00:11:23.170 --> 00:11:28.900
Custer himself, with five companies of about two hundred and ten men, would support Reno.

111
00:11:28.900 --> 00:11:35.110
One more company stayed behind to guard the slow-moving pack train carrying ammunition and supplies.

112
00:11:35.110 --> 00:11:41.960
So the regiment was actually split into four, but it was three separate pieces that would decide the outcome of the battle.

113
00:11:41.960 --> 00:11:45.130
And none of them knew exactly where the others were.

114
00:11:45.130 --> 00:11:52.940
At around three o'clock, Reno crossed the river and galloped across the open valley toward the southern end of the village, toward the Hunkpapa lodges.

115
00:11:52.940 --> 00:11:56.910
The village's first reaction was surprise and panic.

116
00:11:56.910 --> 00:12:00.880
Women snatched up their children, and the elderly fled north.

117
00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:09.810
In those first minutes, the Hunkpapa warrior Gall, who would go on to become one of the leading figures of the battle, lost two of his wives and three of his children.

118
00:12:09.810 --> 00:12:15.540
But attacking a crowded camp turned out to be something very different from what Reno had expected.

119
00:12:15.540 --> 00:12:22.070
A few hundred meters short of the village, seeing the warriors in front of him multiplying fast, he halted.

120
00:12:22.070 --> 00:12:25.080
He ordered his men to dismount and spread out.

121
00:12:25.080 --> 00:12:29.530
The cavalry unit became a scattered line of infantry on open ground.

122
00:12:29.530 --> 00:12:35.740
Because one soldier in every four had to stay back to hold the horses, their firepower dropped even further.

123
00:12:35.740 --> 00:12:43.710
When the warriors began to sweep around the left end of the line, the soldiers fell back to the stand of trees along the riverbank on their right.

124
00:12:43.710 --> 00:12:47.520
The timber was a place that could have been defended well.

125
00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:50.850
But here, disorder turned into disaster.

126
00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:59.620
As Reno stood beside the Arikara scout Bloody Knife, a bullet struck the scout in the head, and Bloody Knife's blood splattered across the major's face.

127
00:12:59.620 --> 00:13:07.270
According to eyewitness accounts, Reno then gave a string of contradictory orders: mount, dismount, then mount again.

128
00:13:07.270 --> 00:13:11.720
Then he galloped out of the timber, heading for the bluffs across the river.

129
00:13:11.720 --> 00:13:15.530
Not everyone had heard the order.

130
00:13:15.530 --> 00:13:21.420
Some soldiers stayed behind in the trees, while the rest followed him in a disorderly mob.

131
00:13:21.420 --> 00:13:24.910
The retreat became a hunt for the warriors.

132
00:13:24.910 --> 00:13:29.680
Riders galloped alongside the fleeing soldiers and knocked them from their saddles.

133
00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:33.170
The river crossing was steep and narrow.

134
00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:39.540
Horses shoved against one another to plunge into the water, then struggled to climb the slope on the far bank.

135
00:13:39.540 --> 00:13:47.670
By the time Reno got from the timber to the hilltop, he had lost about forty of his men, and many others were wounded or trapped back in the trees.

136
00:13:47.670 --> 00:13:53.720
Those who made it to the top, gasping for breath and half in shock, tried to form a defensive line.

137
00:13:53.720 --> 00:13:59.290
Meanwhile, Benteen had scouted the empty hills he had been assigned and turned back.

138
00:13:59.290 --> 00:14:02.620
On the way, he met a messenger from Custer.

139
00:14:02.620 --> 00:14:14.110
The note, hastily scribbled by Custer's adjutant, William Cooke, was very short: it told Benteen to come on, that the village was big, to be quick, and to bring the ammunition packs.

140
00:14:14.110 --> 00:14:18.880
That note was the last written message to come out of Custer's command.

141
00:14:18.880 --> 00:14:27.010
The man who carried it, the Italian-born trumpeter Giovanni Martini, would also be one of the last soldiers to see Custer alive.

142
00:14:27.010 --> 00:14:30.660
Benteen reached Reno's hill at around four o'clock.

143
00:14:30.660 --> 00:14:33.350
There he found a shattered command.

144
00:14:33.350 --> 00:14:37.320
Gunfire could be heard to the north, farther down the valley.

145
00:14:37.320 --> 00:14:46.570
One of the officers, Captain Thomas Weir, moved north with his company without waiting for orders and climbed a hill that would later bear his name, Weir Point.

146
00:14:46.570 --> 00:14:53.740
Several kilometers away in the distance, he saw clouds of dust and smoke, and riders moving across the hills.

147
00:14:53.740 --> 00:14:57.710
But by then, the warriors were turning toward him.

148
00:14:57.710 --> 00:15:02.000
When the other companies came up behind him, the force pulled back.

149
00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:04.530
And the gunfire had stopped.

150
00:15:04.530 --> 00:15:09.140
No soldier could ever tell what Custer's five companies did that afternoon.

151
00:15:09.140 --> 00:15:12.310
Because not a single one of them survived.

152
00:15:12.310 --> 00:15:21.400
What we know comes from two sources: the accounts passed down over the years by the Lakota and Cheyenne who fought there, and the ground itself.

153
00:15:21.400 --> 00:15:28.250
By the time Reno rode down into the valley, Custer had moved north along the high bluffs east of the river.

154
00:15:28.250 --> 00:15:33.020
He saw the village from above, and only then did he grasp how big it was.

155
00:15:33.020 --> 00:15:42.590
Perhaps he was planning to strike the northern end of the village and capture the fleeing families; taking women and children hostage was a known tactic the army had used in the Plains wars.

156
00:15:42.590 --> 00:15:48.640
Part of his command rode down toward the river through a dry creek bed that would later be called Medicine Tail Coulee.

157
00:15:48.640 --> 00:15:53.570
But under fire from the opposite bank, they turned back before they could cross.

158
00:15:53.570 --> 00:15:58.500
Meanwhile, the warriors who had driven back Reno's attack were streaming north.

159
00:15:58.500 --> 00:16:01.830
Gall was pressing in from the south and the east.

160
00:16:01.830 --> 00:16:08.200
Two Moons and Lame White Man, leaders of the Northern Cheyenne, had joined the attack as well.

161
00:16:08.200 --> 00:16:16.490
And according to Native accounts, Crazy Horse led a large body of warriors in a wide arc to the north and came around behind the soldiers.

162
00:16:16.490 --> 00:16:24.620
The five companies, strung out along the ridges, were spread across a line several kilometers long, and they were being surrounded on every side.

163
00:16:24.620 --> 00:16:27.310
The terrain favored the warriors.

164
00:16:27.310 --> 00:16:33.520
Dry creek beds, ravines and tall grass let them close in on the soldiers without being seen.

165
00:16:33.520 --> 00:16:36.210
Many of them used bows.

166
00:16:36.210 --> 00:16:40.980
Arrows loosed upward on a high arc could rain down on the soldiers from out of sight.

167
00:16:40.980 --> 00:16:46.550
Some also carried repeating rifles that fired faster than the army's single-shot carbines.

168
00:16:46.550 --> 00:16:51.480
The soldiers' Model eighteen seventy-three Springfield carbines had a long range.

169
00:16:51.480 --> 00:16:58.170
But as they heated up, spent cartridge cases sometimes jammed in the chamber and had to be pried out with a knife.

170
00:16:58.170 --> 00:17:02.620
Historians still debate how much this affected the outcome of the battle.

171
00:17:02.620 --> 00:17:06.750
The story the ground had to tell came to light in nineteen eighty-three.

172
00:17:06.750 --> 00:17:11.680
That summer, a brush fire burned the grass off the battlefield entirely.

173
00:17:11.680 --> 00:17:18.370
Teams led by the archaeologists Richard Fox and Douglas Scott swept the site inch by inch with metal detectors.

174
00:17:18.370 --> 00:17:22.020
They found thousands of bullets and cartridge cases.

175
00:17:22.020 --> 00:17:30.150
Because each weapon's firing pin left its own distinctive mark on a cartridge case, they could trace how individual guns moved across the battlefield.

176
00:17:30.150 --> 00:17:36.200
The picture that emerged did not match the orderly last-stand scene painted hundreds of times in American art.

177
00:17:36.200 --> 00:17:42.570
Companies had held their ground in one place, then broken apart and fled toward one another in small groups.

178
00:17:42.570 --> 00:17:47.500
The findings largely agreed with the accounts of Native American eyewitnesses.

179
00:17:47.500 --> 00:17:53.710
Those accounts described the battle collapsing into a rout within the final half hour, perhaps even less.

180
00:17:53.710 --> 00:18:02.800
On the southern ridge, the company of Captain Myles Keogh and the company of Lieutenant James Calhoun, Custer's brother-in-law, gave way one after the other.

181
00:18:02.800 --> 00:18:07.090
The survivors ran north, toward the hill where Custer stood.

182
00:18:07.090 --> 00:18:10.740
That hill would later be known as Last Stand Hill.

183
00:18:10.740 --> 00:18:15.030
There, about forty soldiers shot their horses and used them as breastworks.

184
00:18:15.030 --> 00:18:19.160
Lame White Man was killed in one of these final assaults.

185
00:18:19.160 --> 00:18:26.330
When the resistance on the hill collapsed, some soldiers fled toward the river, into a ravine, and died there.

186
00:18:26.330 --> 00:18:38.780
Among the dead were five members of the Custer family: George Armstrong Custer; his brother Tom Custer, a two-time recipient of the Medal of Honor; his younger brother Boston, who had joined the expedition as a civilian;

187
00:18:38.780 --> 00:18:44.350
his eighteen-year-old nephew, Autie Reed; and his brother-in-law, James Calhoun.

188
00:18:44.350 --> 00:18:52.160
Also killed was Mark Kellogg, the correspondent who had joined the expedition hoping to get his dispatches to the newspapers back East.

189
00:18:52.160 --> 00:18:55.970
Custer himself had been shot in the chest and in the left temple.

190
00:18:55.970 --> 00:19:01.060
To the south, on Reno's hill, the siege went on through that evening and all the next day.

191
00:19:01.060 --> 00:19:08.390
Sheltering behind dead horses and supply boxes, the soldiers suffered from thirst and from the fire of sharpshooters.

192
00:19:08.390 --> 00:19:10.920
The wounded begged for water.

193
00:19:10.920 --> 00:19:16.490
A few volunteers made their way down to the river under the warriors' fire and filled canteens.

194
00:19:16.490 --> 00:19:21.260
Most of the Medals of Honor awarded for this battle went to these water carriers.

195
00:19:21.260 --> 00:19:26.510
On the afternoon of June twenty-sixth, the village began to pack up.

196
00:19:26.510 --> 00:19:30.800
Scouts had brought word that a new army was approaching from the north.

197
00:19:30.800 --> 00:19:37.490
The women took down the lodges, and a procession stretching for miles moved south toward the Bighorn Mountains.

198
00:19:37.490 --> 00:19:42.740
On the morning of June twenty-seventh, the column under Terry and Gibbon reached the valley.

199
00:19:42.740 --> 00:19:49.270
Gibbon's chief of scouts, Lieutenant James Bradley, saw scattered white shapes on the ridges.

200
00:19:49.270 --> 00:19:54.840
As he drew closer, he realized they were the bodies of soldiers, stripped of their clothing.

201
00:19:54.840 --> 00:20:04.250
Two hundred sixty-eight men of the Seventh Cavalry, its attached scouts and civilians had been killed; not a single soldier from Custer's five companies survived.

202
00:20:04.250 --> 00:20:09.820
The only living thing to come off that field was Keogh's badly wounded horse, Comanche.

203
00:20:09.820 --> 00:20:15.070
Comanche was later cared for as the regiment's symbol, and no one ever rode him again.

204
00:20:15.070 --> 00:20:19.200
Lakota and Cheyenne losses are not known for certain.

205
00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:23.490
Most estimates put the number somewhere between thirty and one hundred.

206
00:20:23.490 --> 00:20:27.460
When the news reached the East, America was celebrating.

207
00:20:27.460 --> 00:20:34.310
July fourth, eighteen seventy-six, marked the one hundredth anniversary of the Declaration of Independence.

208
00:20:34.310 --> 00:20:40.520
A great world's fair had opened in Philadelphia, showing off the nation's industry and progress to the world.

209
00:20:40.520 --> 00:20:48.170
A few days later, the newspapers reported that the country's most famous cavalryman and five of his companies had been wiped out.

210
00:20:48.170 --> 00:20:50.700
Shock quickly turned to rage.

211
00:20:50.700 --> 00:20:55.470
Congress promptly set aside funds to send new regiments to the plains.

212
00:20:55.470 --> 00:20:59.120
New forts began to rise across the northern plains.

213
00:20:59.120 --> 00:21:05.490
The victory at the Little Bighorn was the most brilliant military day in the history of the Lakota and Cheyenne.

214
00:21:05.490 --> 00:21:09.620
But it brought them nothing that would last even until the following spring.

215
00:21:09.620 --> 00:21:13.750
The great camp would have had to break up by the end of summer anyway.

216
00:21:13.750 --> 00:21:17.720
No single valley could feed that many people and horses for long.

217
00:21:17.720 --> 00:21:22.010
As the bands separated, the army found it easier to pursue them.

218
00:21:22.010 --> 00:21:28.380
Through the winter, the army found the camps, burned their stores of food and seized their horse herds.

219
00:21:28.380 --> 00:21:31.710
The buffalo herds, too, were vanishing fast.

220
00:21:31.710 --> 00:21:35.840
Hunger accomplished what the warriors had not allowed the soldiers to do.

221
00:21:35.840 --> 00:21:44.770
Those who remembered the warning at the Sun Dance would always stress one thing afterward: after the battle, the weapons, horses and belongings of the dead had been taken.

222
00:21:44.770 --> 00:21:50.340
Some Lakota would come to see this plunder as one of the causes of the disasters that followed.

223
00:21:50.340 --> 00:21:54.470
August eighteen seventy-six, Congress passed a law.

224
00:21:54.470 --> 00:22:02.120
Under it, no rations would be handed out at the reservation agencies until the Lakota agreed to give up the Black Hills and their hunting grounds.

225
00:22:02.120 --> 00:22:10.250
In the years that followed, this policy came to be known as "sell or starve." In September, a government commission arrived at the agencies.

226
00:22:10.250 --> 00:22:20.940
The Fort Laramie Treaty required the signatures of three quarters of all adult men for any transfer of land, but the commission managed to collect signatures from only some of the chiefs and leading men.

227
00:22:20.940 --> 00:22:26.030
Congress made the agreement law in February eighteen seventy-seven.

228
00:22:26.030 --> 00:22:30.640
The Black Hills now officially belonged to the United States.

229
00:22:30.640 --> 00:22:34.130
The leaders of the war also met their end in these years.

230
00:22:34.130 --> 00:22:43.060
In May eighteen seventy-seven, Crazy Horse came in to Camp Robinson in Nebraska with some nine hundred of his starving people and surrendered.

231
00:22:43.060 --> 00:22:50.870
In September of that same year, he was stabbed with a bayonet in a scuffle with soldiers who were trying to lock him up, and he died that night.

232
00:22:50.870 --> 00:22:56.600
Sitting Bull, for his part, crossed north into Canada with some of his people.

233
00:22:56.600 --> 00:23:05.050
But there were no buffalo there either, and in eighteen eighty-one he came in to Fort Buford in Montana with about two hundred people and laid down his arms.

234
00:23:05.050 --> 00:23:14.780
Nine years later, in December eighteen ninety, he was killed outside his home on the Standing Rock reservation, in a clash with the Lakota policemen who had come to arrest him.

235
00:23:14.780 --> 00:23:18.750
The echoes of the Little Bighorn inside the army lasted a long time.

236
00:23:18.750 --> 00:23:26.080
Custer's widow, Elizabeth Custer, spent the rest of her life guarding her husband's heroic image and wrote books about him.

237
00:23:26.080 --> 00:23:30.530
Those who placed the blame elsewhere usually pointed at Reno and Benteen.

238
00:23:30.530 --> 00:23:38.820
At Reno's own request, a military court of inquiry met in eighteen seventy-nine and found no evidence strong enough to charge him with cowardice.

239
00:23:38.820 --> 00:23:41.670
But the argument never ended.

240
00:23:41.670 --> 00:23:49.480
To this day, those who defend Custer's decisions still argue with those who fault him for splitting the regiment and for attacking instead of waiting.

241
00:23:49.480 --> 00:23:53.450
The story of the Black Hills did not end in eighteen seventy-seven.

242
00:23:53.450 --> 00:24:00.940
From the very first day, the Lakota insisted that the transfer was illegal, and they pursued the case in the courts for decades.

243
00:24:00.940 --> 00:24:09.870
Meanwhile, between nineteen twenty-seven and nineteen forty-one, the giant faces of four American presidents were carved into the granite of the Black Hills.

244
00:24:09.870 --> 00:24:18.000
Today this monument, known as Mount Rushmore, stands in mountains the Lakota hold sacred, and draws millions of visitors every year.

245
00:24:18.000 --> 00:24:22.610
In nineteen eighty, the case reached the United States Supreme Court.

246
00:24:22.610 --> 00:24:29.300
By a vote of eight to one, the Court ruled that the Black Hills had been wrongfully taken from the Lakota.

247
00:24:29.300 --> 00:24:40.310
According to the Court, the land seized in eighteen seventy-seven had been worth seventeen point five million dollars, and interest running from eighteen seventy-seven onward had to be added to that sum.

248
00:24:40.310 --> 00:24:44.120
The total award came to more than one hundred million dollars.

249
00:24:44.120 --> 00:24:47.770
The Lakota tribes, however, refused the money.

250
00:24:47.770 --> 00:24:54.300
To them, accepting it would mean agreeing that the land had been sold, when what they wanted was the land itself.

251
00:24:54.300 --> 00:25:00.990
Ever since, the money has sat in a government account, and with interest it has long since passed one billion dollars.

252
00:25:00.990 --> 00:25:06.400
Meanwhile, the Lakota reservations remain among the poorest places in the United States.

253
00:25:06.400 --> 00:25:11.970
A people with a billion-dollar account set aside in their name will not touch it, as a matter of principle.

254
00:25:11.970 --> 00:25:15.620
The battlefield itself changed over time.

255
00:25:15.620 --> 00:25:21.670
For many years it carried Custer's name, and the only monument there honored the dead of the Seventh Cavalry.

256
00:25:21.670 --> 00:25:33.320
In nineteen ninety-one, the United States Congress renamed the site the Little Bighorn Battlefield National Monument and decided that a memorial should also be built for the Native Americans who fought there.

257
00:25:33.320 --> 00:25:36.490
That memorial opened in two thousand three.

258
00:25:36.490 --> 00:25:46.540
Today, on the ridges, among the white marble markers showing where soldiers fell, there also stand red granite markers showing where Lakota and Cheyenne warriors died.

259
00:25:46.540 --> 00:25:53.070
Every year, in the last days of June, riders gather in the valley of the Greasy Grass to remember the battle.

260
00:25:53.070 --> 00:26:02.000
A battle won in a few hours of a single afternoon, and a land that has not been won back in a century and a half, stand side by side in this small valley.

261
00:26:02.000 --> 00:26:06.290
That brings us to the end of today's episode of Nöron Tarih.

262
00:26:06.290 --> 00:26:08.980
Until we meet again in a new story.
